title: A tour of Bayan
nav: Start
order: 18
template: docs
---
<p class="lede">Twenty-four steps: one idea, one complete program and the compiler's output each, run with <code>bayan run</code>.</p>

<p>Install the compiler first: <a href="install.html">Install Bayan</a>. The <a href="reading.html">reading guide</a> goes deeper on forms you have not seen.</p>

<ol class="steps">
  <li>
    <h2 id="hello">1. Hello</h2>
    <p><code>main</code> starts the program; <code>print</code> writes a line. Newlines end statements, so there are no semicolons.</p>
    <example name="tour-hello"/>
  </li>
  <li>
    <h2 id="bindings">2. let and var</h2>
    <p><code>let</code> binds a name that never changes, <code>var</code> one you can assign to; types are inferred from the value.</p>
    <example name="tour-bindings"/>
  </li>
  <li>
    <h2 id="types">3. Basic types</h2>
    <p>Numbers are sized: <code>i64</code> (64-bit integer), <code>f64</code> (64-bit float), plus <code>bool</code> and <code>string</code>. Write a type to pin one down.</p>
    <example name="tour-types"/>
  </li>
  <li>
    <h2 id="functions">4. Functions</h2>
    <p>Signature order: return type, name, then parameters, each type first. <code>return</code> sends a value back; <code>void</code> means none.</p>
    <example name="tour-functions"/>
  </li>
  <li>
    <h2 id="expression">5. One-line functions</h2>
    <p>A one-expression body can use <code>=&gt;</code> instead of braces and <code>return</code>; read it as "evaluates to".</p>
    <example name="tour-expression"/>
  </li>
  <li>
    <h2 id="if">6. Conditions</h2>
    <p>Conditions have no parentheses; bodies always have braces. <code>else if</code> chains further checks.</p>
    <example name="tour-if"/>
  </li>
  <li>
    <h2 id="loops">7. Loops</h2>
    <p><code>for</code> walks a range or collection: <code>1..=3</code> includes the 3, <code>1..3</code> stops before it. <code>while</code> repeats until its condition is false.</p>
    <example name="tour-loops"/>
  </li>
  <li>
    <h2 id="postfix">8. A condition at the end</h2>
    <p>A statement can end with <code>if</code> or <code>unless</code>: a sentence instead of a block, on <code>return</code>, <code>fail</code>, <code>break</code>, <code>continue</code>, calls and assignments.</p>
    <example name="tour-postfix"/>
  </li>
  <li>
    <h2 id="strings">9. Joining text</h2>
    <p>Strings have no <code>+</code>: put <code>$</code> before the quote and expressions in braces; a named argument such as <code>as: .hex</code> picks a format.</p>
    <example name="tour-strings"/>
  </li>
  <li>
    <h2 id="lists">10. Lists</h2>
    <p><code>List&lt;T&gt;</code> grows: <code>append</code> adds at the end, <code>length</code> counts, <code>xs[1]</code> reads by position. A list you change must be a <code>var</code>.</p>
    <example name="tour-lists"/>
  </li>
  <li>
    <h2 id="maps">11. Maps</h2>
    <p><code>Map&lt;K, V&gt;</code> maps keys to values in insertion order. <code>tryGet(at:)</code> returns an optional; <code>??</code> supplies the value when the key is missing.</p>
    <example name="tour-maps"/>
  </li>
  <li>
    <h2 id="structs">12. Structs</h2>
    <p>A struct groups fields, each typed first, with an optional default. <code>Point { x: 3 }</code> leaves the rest at their defaults.</p>
    <example name="tour-structs"/>
  </li>
  <li>
    <h2 id="methods">13. Methods and ref this</h2>
    <p>A method is read-only unless its first parameter is <code>ref this</code>; the signature shows <code>bump</code> changes the counter and <code>isBusy</code> does not. Changing methods need a <code>var</code>.</p>
    <example name="tour-methods"/>
  </li>
  <li>
    <h2 id="with">14. Copy and update</h2>
    <p><code>a with { qty: 5 }</code> copies <code>a</code> with the listed fields replaced; the original stays as it was.</p>
    <example name="tour-with"/>
  </li>
  <li>
    <h2 id="enums">15. Enums and .member</h2>
    <p>An enum is a fixed set of names; where the type is known, a leading dot names a member: <code>.green</code> means <code>Color.green</code>. A <code>switch</code> on an enum must cover every member.</p>
    <example name="tour-enums"/>
  </li>
  <li>
    <h2 id="unions">16. Unions</h2>
    <p>A union is one of several cases, each with its own data. <code>switch</code> destructures the case with <code>let</code>, and the compiler rejects a missing case.</p>
    <example name="tour-unions"/>
  </li>
  <li>
    <h2 id="optionals">17. Optionals</h2>
    <p>A plain type is never null; <code>string?</code> is a string or nothing. After <code>is not null</code> it is plain again; <code>??</code> gives a fallback.</p>
    <example name="tour-optionals"/>
  </li>
  <li>
    <h2 id="particles">18. Particles</h2>
    <p>A required parameter after the first carries a role word (<code>from</code>, <code>to</code>, <code>until</code>, <code>through</code>, <code>at</code>, <code>by</code>, <code>for</code>, <code>as</code>), repeated at the call, so swapped accounts show.</p>
    <example name="tour-particles"/>
  </li>
  <li>
    <h2 id="lambdas">19. Lambdas and it</h2>
    <p>Where a function is expected, pass a lambda: <code>x =&gt; x % 2 == 1</code>. One argument can be <code>it</code>, skipping the parameter. <code>Pred</code> names <code>i32 -&gt; bool</code>.</p>
    <example name="tour-lambdas"/>
  </li>
  <li>
    <h2 id="errors">20. Errors are values</h2>
    <p><code>fails</code> lists a function's errors; <code>fail</code> leaves with one, <code>try</code> passes it up, <code>catch</code> takes a fallback value or an exhaustive <code>catch switch</code>. No exceptions.</p>
    <example name="tour-errors"/>
  </li>
  <li>
    <h2 id="defer">21. defer</h2>
    <p><code>defer</code> runs a statement when the scope ends, however it ends, in reverse order: use it to release what you opened.</p>
    <example name="tour-defer"/>
  </li>
  <li>
    <h2 id="effects">22. Effects with uses</h2>
    <p>A public function declares what it does besides computing: <code>uses alloc</code> allocates, no <code>uses</code> is pure; a missing needed effect does not compile.</p>
    <example name="tour-effects"/>
  </li>
  <li>
    <h2 id="requires">23. requires</h2>
    <p>A <code>requires</code> rule must hold for every value of the type; a breaking value cannot be built, and construction can fail, so it takes <code>try</code> or <code>catch</code>.</p>
    <example name="tour-requires"/>
  </li>
  <li>
    <h2 id="move">24. Moving a list</h2>
    <p>A list is owned by one name; <code>move</code> hands it to another and the old name stops working. To keep both, ask for <code>.copy()</code>.</p>
    <example name="tour-move"/>
  </li>
</ol>

<h2 id="next">Where next</h2>
<p>Next: <a href="reading.html">Reading Bayan code</a>, <a href="features.html">What makes Bayan different</a>, <a href="errors.html">Errors</a> and <a href="memory.html">Memory and ownership</a>.</p>
